In Java programming language, access modifiers like `public`, `protected`, or `private` control the visibility and accessibility of class members, including methods.

The keyword `public` in method declarations specifies that the method has **public accessibility,** meaning anyone can access that method from anywhere within the same project. 

Methods with public accessibility are visible and accessible outside their declaring class, even by classes located in different packages (depending on package access rules). This allows clients outside the current object instance to invoke the method directly and make use of its functionality without having to know anything about the inner workings of the class. 

